gles that span multiple rows, covering multiple rows' left intervals in one rectangle? For example, suppose we have rows r1 and r2 with uncovered squares at columns c1 and c2. If c1 and c2 are both > some column, maybe we can have a rectangle covering columns 1..min(c1,c2)-1 across rows r1 and r2. That rectangle would be a left rectangle for both rows. But the solution's lower bound says a rectangle can be a left rectangle for both rows only if d < min(p(r1), p(r2)), where d is the rightmost column of the rectangle. So if we choose rectangle covering columns 1..d, where d < min(p(r1), p(r2)), then it's left rectangle for both rows. However, the solution claims that this can happen only if those rows share the same p value. Wait, they claim: \"Thus a single rectangle can serve as a left (or right) rectangle for several rows only when those rows share the same value of p. Since p is a permutation, the values p(r) are all distinct; consequently each interior row (p(r)≠1,n) needs its own left rectangle and its own right rectangle.\"\n\nBut this is wrong: The condition for a rectangle to be left rectangle for both rows is d < min(p(r1), p(r2)), i.e., the rectangle's rightmost column is less than both p(r1) and p(r2). That does not require p(r1) = p(r2). For example, if p(r1) = 5 and p(r2) = 7, we can have a rectangle covering columns 1..4 across rows r1 and r2. That's left rectangle for both rows, even though p values differ. So the solution's lower bound argument is flawed. Indeed, we can combine left intervals of multiple rows into one rectangle, as long as we restrict to the columns left of the smallest p among those rows. Similarly for right intervals: we can combine across rows if we restrict to columns right of the largest p among those rows.\n\nThus the lower bound of 2n-2 is not necessarily minimal. Could we do better? Possibly yes. Let's think about the problem: we have a permutation matrix of holes; we need to cover the rest with rectangles. This is reminiscent