in σ(i). Then the rectangle covers columns 1..m across rows R. This rectangle is valid (covers only left side of each row's hole). Similarly for right intervals: choose columns > max σ(i). So we can combine many rows into one rectangle if we restrict to columns that are left of all holes in those rows (or right of all holes). So we can cover many left intervals with a single rectangle by using columns up to the smallest hole among those rows.\n\nThus the lower bound argument that each interior row needs its own left rectangle is false. The minimal number of rectangles might be less than 2n-2.\n\nBut is there a lower bound that matches something else? Let's try to find a lower bound: Each row has at most one uncovered square, and each column has exactly one uncovered square. The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix. The complement is a bipartite graph? Actually, each rectangle can be thought of as covering a submatrix of the complement. The problem is essentially to partition the complement of a permutation matrix into rectangles. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a matrix with a given pattern is a known combinatorial problem: the rectangle covering number (or partition number) of a matrix. For a permutation matrix, the complement is a matrix with all 1's except zeros on the permutation positions. The rectangle partition number of the complement is known to be 2n-1? Let's check known results: There's a known problem: \"Cover the cells of an n×n board with non-overlapping rectangles such that each row and column contains exactly one uncovered cell.\" The minimal number of rectangles is 2n-2? Or maybe it's n+⌈n/2⌉? Not sure.\n\nLet's try to find a better construction: Suppose we have a permutation matrix with holes at positions (i,i) (i.e., identity). Then each row i has hole at column i. The complement consists of all cells where column ≠ row. We can partition this complement into n rectangles: For each i, take rectangle covering rows i..n an