he cells \\((i,1),(i,2),\\dots ,(i,i-1)\\).\n\n   This uses exactly \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles, all of which avoid the diagonal cells, and together they cover every other unit square exactly once.\n\n5. **Conclusion** \u2013  \n   The lower bound matches the explicit construction, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) we obtain \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe work with an \\(n\\times n\\) board whose unit squares are indexed by \\((r,c)\\) with\n\\(1\\le r,c\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Form of the uncovered squares  \n\nEach row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square.  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a bijection\n\\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nPermuting rows by the permutation \\(\\pi\\) and columns by \\(\\sigma\\) does not change the\nnumber of tiles needed, because the whole configuration is merely relabelled.\nHence we may assume, without loss of generality, that  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)=i\\qquad\\text{for all }i,\n\\]\n\ni.e. the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Tiles as complete bipartite subgraphs  \n\nA rectangular tile that occupies rows \\(R\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and columns\n\\(C\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (with \\(R\\) and \\(C\\) non\u2011empty) covers exactly the set  \n\n\\[\nT(R,C)=\\{(r,c)\\mid r\\in R,\\;c\\in C\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus a tile corresponds to the complete bipartite graph \\(K_{R,C}\\) between the\nrow\u2011vertices \\(R\\) and the column\u2011vertices \\(C\\).\n\nThe uncovered diagonal cells form the perfect matching  \n\n\\[\nM=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince a tile may not contain any uncovered cell, each tile must be a complete\nbipartite subgraph of the graph  \n\n\\[\nG = K_{n,n}\\setminus M .\n\\]\n\nConsequently the family of tiles gives a *partition* of the edge\u2011set of \\(G\\)\ninto complete bipartite subgraphs (bicliques).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nConsider two consecutive rows \\(i-1\\) and \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\).\nLook at the two off\u2011diagonal cells\n\n\\[\na_i=(i,i-1),\\qquad b_i=(i-1,i).\n\\]\n\nBoth belong to the complement \\(G\\) because they are not on the diagonal.\n\n*Claim.*  The cells \\(a_i\\) and \\(b_i\\) cannot lie in the same tile.\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nSuppose a tile \\(T(R,C)\\) contains \\(a_i\\). Then \\(i\\in R\\) and \\(i-1\\in C\\).\nIf the same tile also contained \\(b_i\\), we would have \\(i-1\\in R\\) and\n\\(i\\in C\\). Hence both rows \\(i-1\\) and \\(i\\) would belong to \\(R\\) and both\ncolumns \\(i-1\\) and \\(i\\) would belong to \\(C\\). Consequently the tile would also\ncontain the diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\) (or \\((i-1,i-1)\\)), contradicting the\nrequirement that no tile may cover an uncovered square. \u220e\n\nThus for each \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\) the two cells \\(a_i\\) and \\(b_i\\) must be assigned\nto **different** tiles. Hence we need at least two distinct tiles for each of the\n\\(n-1\\) adjacent row pairs. Therefore any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n2\\,(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nWe now exhibit a construction that meets the lower bound.\n\nBecause the uncovered cells are \\((i,i)\\), the board splits naturally into an\nupper\u2011triangular region (cells with column\u202f>\u202frow) and a lower\u2011triangular region\n(cells with column\u202f<\u202frow).\n\n*Upper\u2011triangular tiles.*  \nFor each row \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) place one horizontal rectangle\n\n\\[\nU_i = \\{(i,c)\\mid c=i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(U_i\\) lies entirely above the diagonal, so it avoids the uncovered cell\n\\((i,i)\\). The sets \\(U_1,\\dots ,U_{n-1}\\) are disjoint and together cover every\ncell of the upper triangle.\n\n*Lower\u2011triangular tiles.*  \nFor each row \\(i=2,3,\\dots ,n\\) place one horizontal rectangle\n\n\\[\nL_i = \\{(i,c)\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,i-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) lies entirely below the diagonal, thus avoids \\((i,i)\\). The\nrectangles \\(L_2,\\dots ,L_n\\) are disjoint and together cover the whole lower\ntriangle.\n\nThe total number of rectangles is  \n\n\\[\n|U|+|L| = (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nEvery unit square that is not on the diagonal belongs to exactly one of these\nrectangles, and each diagonal square remains uncovered. Hence this is a valid\ntiling using \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Minimality  \n\nSection\u202f3 proved that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while\nSection\u202f4 provides a tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles. Consequently the\nminimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2n-2}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus the answer to the problem is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).**"
