raph \\(K_{R,C}\\) between the\nrow‑vertices \\(R\\) and the column‑vertices \\(C\\).\n\nThe uncovered diagonal cells form the perfect matching  \n\n\\[\nM=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nSince a tile may not contain any uncovered cell, each tile must be a biclique\ncontained in  \n\n\\[\nG = K_{n,n}\\setminus M .\n\\]\n\nConsequently a tiling of the board is exactly a **partition** of the edge‑set of\n\\(G\\) into complete bipartite subgraphs (bicliques).  The problem therefore asks\nfor the bipartite dimension of \\(G\\), i.e. the smallest number of bicliques whose\nedges partition \\(E(G)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\)\n\nConsider two consecutive rows \\(i-1\\) and \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\).  \nExamine the two off‑diagonal cells\n\n\\[\na_i=(i,i-1),\\qquad b_i=(i-1,i).\n\\]\n\nBoth belong to \\(E(G)\\) because they are not on the diagonal.\n\n*Claim.*  The cells \\(a_i\\) and \\(b_i\\) cannot lie in the same biclique of a\npartition of \\(E(G)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a biclique \\(K_{R,C}\\) contains \\(a_i\\).  Then \\(i\\in R\\) and\n\\(i-1\\in C\\).  If the same biclique also contained \\(b_i\\), we would have\n\\(i-1\\in R\\) and \\(i\\in C\\) as well.  Hence both rows \\(i-1,i\\) and both columns\n\\(i-1,i\\) would belong to the biclique, which would force the diagonal edge\n\\((i,i)\\) (or \\((i-1,i-1)\\)) to be present—contradicting the definition of\n\\(G\\) as the complement of the matching \\(M\\).  ∎\n\nThus for each \\(i=2,\\dots ,n\\) the two cells \\(a_i\\) and \\(b_i\\) must be assigned\nto **different** bicliques.  There are \\(n-1\\) such adjacent‑row pairs, so any\npartition of \\(E(G)\\) requires at least  \n\n\\[\n2\\,(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\nbicliques, i.e. at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A tiling using exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nWe now exhibit a concrete tiling that mee