d (explicit construction).**  \n   Again assume the uncovered squares are \\((i,i)\\).  \n   For each \\(k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\) place two tiles:\n\n   * \\(T_k^{\\text{up}}:\\) rows \\(1,\\dots ,k\\) and columns \\(k+1,\\dots ,n\\)\n     (the “upper‑right” rectangle);\n   * \\(T_k^{\\text{down}}:\\) rows \\(k+1,\\dots ,n\\) and columns \\(1,\\dots ,k\\)\n     (the “lower‑left” rectangle).\n\n   The two families are pairwise disjoint and together they cover every cell\n   \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  Exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles are used.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the construction achieving it coincide, so the\n   minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe are given an \\(n\\times n\\) board of unit squares.  \nA *tile* is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; the\ntiles are pairwise interior‑disjoint.  \nExactly one square in each row and each column must remain uncovered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Reduction to a diagonal pattern  \n\nPermuting rows and columns does not affect the problem, because the set of\nuncovered squares must contain exactly one element from each row and each column,\nhence it is a permutation matrix.  \nThus we may relabel the rows and columns so that the uncovered squares are the\nmain diagonal cells  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other squares have to be covered by tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Tiles as complete bipartite subgraphs  \n\nIdentify the set of rows with a left vertex class \\(R=\\{r_1,\\dots ,r_n\\}\\) and the\nset of columns with a right vertex class \\(C=\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_n\\}\\).  \nA tile that occupies the set of rows \\(R'\\subseteq R\\) and 