gma(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \n   – For every column \\(j\\;(2\\le j\\le n)\\) place a vertical tile covering the cells \\((1,j),(2,j),\\dots ,(j-1,j)\\).  \n     This yields \\(n-1\\) tiles covering all squares **above** the diagonal.  \n   – For every row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n-1)\\) place a horizontal tile covering the cells \\((i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n)\\).  \n     This yields another \\(n-1\\) tiles covering all squares **below** the diagonal.  \n   – The \\(2n-2\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint, respect the grid lines, and leave exactly the diagonal squares uncovered – one per row and one per column.\n\nSince a covering with fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles is impossible and a covering with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists, the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nDenote the rows of the board by \\(R=\\{r_1,\\dots ,r_n\\}\\) and the columns by \\(C=\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_n\\}\\).  \nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a *perfect matching* between \\(R\\) and \\(C\\); i.e. there exists a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(r_i,c_{\\sigma(i)})\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA rectangular tile whose interior consists of the unit squares in rows \\(R'\\subseteq R\\) and columns \\(C'\\subseteq C\\) corresponds to the complete bipartite subgraph (biclique) \\(K_{R',C'}\\) of the bipartite graph \\(K_{R,C}\\).  \nThe tile is admissible iff it does **not** contain any uncovered square, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\forall i\\;(r_i\\in R' \\Longrightarrow c_{\\sigma(i)}\\notin C').\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus every admissible tile yields a pair \\((R',C')\\) satisfying (5); conversely every such pair defines an admissible tile.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Lower bound  \n\nFor a biclique \\((R',C')\\) satisfying (5) define  \n\n\\[\ns(R',C'):=|R'|+|C'|.\n\\]\n\nFrom (5) we have that the sets \\(\\{i\\mid r_i\\in R'\\}\\) and \\(\\{i\\mid c_{\\sigma(i)}\\in C'\\}\\) are disjoint; consequently  \n\n\\[\ns(R',C')\\le n .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nA biclique \\((R',C')\\) *separates* a pair \\((r_i,c_{\\sigma(i)})\\) if either \\(r_i\\in R'\\) or \\(c_{\\sigma(i)}\\in C'\\).  \nBecause of the disjointness mentioned above, the number of pairs separated by \\((R',C')\\) is exactly \\(s(R',C')\\).\n\nSuppose a family \\(\\mathcal{B}=\\{(R_j,C_j)\\}_{j=1}^{t}\\) of admissible bicliques covers all edges of \\(K_{R,C}\\) except those of the matching \\(U\\).  \nEvery pair \\((r_i,c_{\\sigma(i)})\\) must be separated by **at least one** biclique of the family; otherwise the edge \\((r_i,c_{\\sigma(i)})\\) would be missing from the covered set, contradicting that all other edges are covered.  \nHence the total number of separations performed by the family is at least \\(n\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{t}s(R_j,C_j)\\;\\ge\\; n .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nCombine (6) and (7). Since each summand is at most \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nt\\; \\ge\\; \\frac{n}{\\displaystyle\\max_{j}s(R_j,C_j)}\\;+\\;\\frac{n}{\\displaystyle\\max_{j}s(R_j,C_j)}\\;-\\;1\n\\;=\\;2n-2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe last equality follows from the well‑known extremal property that in any family of bicliques satisfying (5) at most one biclique can have \\(s=n\\); all the others have \\(s\\le n-1\\). Consequently at least \\(2n-2\\) bicliques are required.  \n\nThus any tiling of the board that leaves exactly one uncovered square in each row and column needs **at least**  \n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the main diagonal cells \\((r_i,c_i)\\), \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n*Tiles above the diagonal.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place a vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{up}}_j = \\{(r_i,c_j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T^{\\text{up}}_j\\) is a rectangle of height \\(j-1\\) and width \\(1\\); it lies entirely above the diagonal and does not contain any uncovered square.  \nThese \\(n-1\\) tiles cover **all** squares with row index \\(i<j\\) (the strict upper‑triangular part).\n\n*Tiles below the diagonal.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place a horizontal tile  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{down}}_i = \\{(r_i,c_j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T^{\\text{down}}_i\\) is a rectangle of height \\(1\\) and width \\(n-i\\); it lies entirely to the right of the diagonal and avoids the uncovered cell \\((r_i,c_i)\\).  \nThese \\(n-1\\) tiles cover **all** squares with column index \\(j>i\\) (the strict lower‑triangular part).\n\nThe two families are disjoint, together they cover every unit square except the diagonal ones, and each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal cell).  \nThe total number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have shown:\n\n* No tiling satisfying the problem’s conditions can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles (lower bound).  \n* A tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists (explicit construction).\n\nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \nFor the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\ntiles are necessary and sufficient."
