                    "reasoning_content": "We need to evaluate the provided solution for the problem: \"Consider a 2025×2025 grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of difference sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that not covered by any tile.\"\n\nThe solution:\n\n- Introduces B = set of coordinates, n = 2025.\n- Defines p(r) = column of uncovered square in row r.\n- Since each column also has exactly one uncovered square, p is a permutation.\n- Lower bound: For each row, if p(r) = 1 or n, need at least one rectangle; if interior, need at least two rectangles (left and right). Then argues that a rectangle can serve as left rectangle for multiple rows only if those rows share same p(r) (since rectangle's column interval must be left of both p(r)s). Since p is a permutation, all p(r) distinct, so each interior row needs its own left rectangle and its own right rectangle. So total rectangles at least (n-2)+(n-2)+2 = 2n-2.\n- Construction: For each row, define left rectangle covering columns 1 to p(r)-1 if p(r)>1, and right rectangle covering columns p(r)+1 to n if p(r)<n. These are height 1 rectangles. They are disjoint across rows. So total rectangles = number of rows with p(r)>1 + number of rows with p(r)<n = (n-1)+(n-1) = 2n-2. So bound is tight.\n- For n=2025, answer 4048.\n\nWe need to check answer correctness: The answer is 4048. Is that correct? Let's think about the problem: We need to cover all unit squares except exactly one uncovered per row and per column. So we need to leave exactly n uncovered squares, one per row and column. That's a permutation of uncovered squares. We need to place rectangular tiles covering all other squares, with no overlap. Tiles can be any rectangles aligned to grid lines