o we could have many rectangles.\n\nThus the lower bound might be more complex.\n\nLet's examine known results: The minimal number of rectangles needed to tile an n x n board with a single missing cell per row and column (i.e., a permutation matrix) is known as the \"rectangular partition of a permutation matrix complement\". This is related to the concept of \"grid representation of permutations\" and \"rectangular decomposition\". There's a known result: The complement of a permutation matrix can be partitioned into at most 2n-2 rectangles, and this bound is tight for the identity permutation (where uncovered squares are on the diagonal). Actually, consider identity permutation: uncovered squares at (i,i). The complement consists of all cells not on the diagonal. We can cover the board with 2n-2 rectangles: n-1 rectangles above the diagonal (each covering a row segment from column i+1 to n) and n-1 rectangles below the diagonal (each covering a row segment from column 1 to i-1). That's exactly the construction given. And the lower bound is also 2n-2 because each row and column must have exactly one uncovered cell, and each rectangle can be either wholly above or wholly below the diagonal? Actually, for identity permutation, each row's uncovered cell is at column i. The left side of row i is columns 1..i-1, and the right side is columns i+1..n. We can combine left sides of multiple rows? For rows i and j with ij, left side of row j includes columns 1..j-1, which includes column i (the uncovered cell of row i). So we cannot have a rectangle covering columns 1..i across rows i and j because that would cover the uncovered cell at (i,i). So left sides cannot be combined across rows if they include the column of an uncovered cell in another row. However, we could combine left sides of rows that are \"consecutive\" in terms of p values? Let's think.\n\nIf p is identity, p(r)=r. For rows r1r2, left side of row r2 includes columns 1..r2-1, which includes column r1 (since r