e. This is not logically implied: distinct p values do not preclude the existence of a rectangle with d less than the minimum of any two p values. For any set of rows, we can pick d min of their p values. Since p values are distinct, the minimum is some value, and we can choose d = min(p)-1 (or less). So we can have a rectangle covering columns 1..(min(p)-1) across all those rows. So the rectangle can serve as left rectangle for all rows whose p values are greater than that min(p). So we can combine many rows into one left rectangle. However, the solution's argument also says that each interior row needs its own left rectangle because each interior row's left rectangle must be distinct. Let's examine if there is any hidden assumption: maybe they assume that the rectangle covering left side for a row must cover all left side squares of that row (i.e., the entire left segment). But the solution's construction uses each left side as a single rectangle per row covering all left squares. However, the lower bound argument might be assuming that any rectangle covering left side squares of a row must cover the entire left side segment (i.e., from column 1 to p(r)-1). If we allow splitting the left side into multiple rectangles, maybe we can reduce the total number of rectangles. But the lower bound argument is about minimal number of rectangles, so we cannot assume that each left side must be covered by a single rectangle. The solution's lower bound argument seems to assume that each interior row's left side must be covered by at least one rectangle, which is true. But they also assume that each interior row's left side requires a distinct rectangle (i.e., cannot share). That's false as we argued: left side segments can be partially overlapped across rows, but they may need multiple rectangles to cover all left side squares. However, maybe the lower bound argument is about the number of rectangles needed to cover the left side squares of all rows, not about each row's left side being covered by a distinct rectangle. They claim each interior row needs its own left rectangle because each rectangle can serve as left rectangle for several rows only if those rows share the same p value. But we found a counterexample: rectangle covering columns 1..c where c min(p(r1), p(r2)) can serve as left rectangle for both rows, even if p values are distinct. So the lower bound argument is flawed.\n\nThus the lower bound of 2n-2 may not be valid. Let's try to find a better lower bound or see if we can find a construction with fewer than 2n-2 rectangles for some permutation.\n\nLet's try to find a construction that uses fewer rectangles for n=5 maybe. Let's try to find a permutation that allows combining left rectangles across many rows. For example, consider permutation p where uncovered squares are all in the first column except one in the last column? But p must be a permutation, so each column appears exactly once. So we cannot have many rows with uncovered squares in the same column. But we can have a permutation where p values are arranged such that many rows have uncovered squares far to the right, allowing a large common left rectangle covering columns 1..c across many rows. For instance, suppose p values are [5,4,3,2,1] for n=5 (reverse identity). Then p(r) = n+1 - r. So p(1)=5, p(2)=4, p(3)=3, p(4)=2, p(5)=1. The left side of row r includes columns 1..p(r)-1. So for row 1, left side is columns 1..4; row 2 left side is columns 1..3; row 3 left side is columns 1..2; row 4 left side is column 1; row 5 left side is empty. So we can have a rectangle covering columns 1..1 across rows 1-4 (i.e., column 1 across rows 1-4). That's allowed because column 1 is not uncovered for rows 1-4 (uncovered at row 5). So that rectangle covers left side squares (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1). Then we need to cover remaining left side squares: for row 1 columns 2..4, row 2 columns 2..3, row 3 column 2, row 4 none. We could have rectangle covering columns 2..2 across rows 1-3 (i.e., column 2 across rows 1-3). That's allowed because column 2 is uncovered at row 4 (p(4)=2). So we cannot include row 4. But we can include rows 1-3. That rectangle covers (1,2),(2,2),(3,2). Then we need to cover column 3 for rows 1-2 (since p(3)=3). So rectangle covering column 3 across rows 1-2 is allowed (uncovered at row 3). Then column 4 across row 1 only. So left side can be covered with 4 rectangles (columns 1 across rows 1-4, column 2 across rows 1-3, column 3 across rows 1-2, column 4 across row1). That's n-1 = 4 rectangles for left side. Similarly, right side can be covered with n-1 rectangles. So total 2n-2 again.\n\nBut maybe we can combine left and right sides across rows to reduce total? For example, we could have a rectangle covering a region that includes both left and right side squares of a row, but not the uncovered square. However, any rectangle that includes both left and right side squares must cross the column of the uncovered square, which would include the uncovered square unless the rectangle's column interval excludes that column. So a rectangle cannot have column interval that includes both columns p(r) and > p(r) without covering p(r). So any rectangle must be entirely to the left or entirely to the right of the uncovered square for each row it covers. So each rectangle is either left-type (its column interval is a subset of columns left of the uncovered squares of all rows it covers) or right-type (subset of columns right of uncovered squares). So we cannot combine left and right sides in a single rectangle.\n\nThus the total number of rectangles is at least the number of left-type rectangles plus right-type rectangles. The lower bound argument tries to bound each of those from below.\n\nNow, can we cover all left-side squares using fewer than n-1 rectangles? For any permutation, the left side squares form a Young diagram shape (Ferrers diagram) of shape determined by p. The left side squares are those (r,c) such that c p(r). This is a set of squares that is monotone: if (r,c) is in left side, then for any r' > r, c p(r')? Not necessarily monotone. Let's think: Since p is a permutation, the set of left side squares is not necessarily monotone in rows. However, we can think of the left side region as a union of columns: For each column c, the rows r where c p(r) are those rows where p(r) > c. Since p is a permutation, the set of rows where p(r) > c is exactly the set of rows whose p value is in {c+1,...,n}. That's a set of size n-c. So column c appears in left side for exactly n-c rows. Those rows are not necessarily contiguous; they are the rows where p(r) > c. Since p is a permutation, the rows where p(r) > c are exactly the rows whose p value is larger than c; the positions of those rows can be scattered. So left side squares in a given column are scattered across rows, not necessarily contiguous. So we cannot have a rectangle covering column c across all those rows because the rows are not contiguous. However, we can have rectangles covering contiguous blocks of rows where column c is left side. So we need to partition each column's left side squares into intervals of rows. The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover left side squares is at least the number of \"runs\" of rows where column c is left side. Since each column c has n-c left side squares, but they may be broken into multiple runs. In worst case, each column could have many runs. For the identity permutation, column c left side squares are rows r > c (since p(r) = r > c). So rows r > c are contiguous (rows c+1 to n). So column c left side squares form a single contiguous block for each column. So we can cover column c left side squares with one rectangle (rows c+1..n, column c). That's exactly the construction that yields n-1 left rectangles (for columns 1..n-1). Similarly, right side squares for identity are rows r c (since p(r) = r c). Those also form contiguous blocks (rows 1..c-1). So we get n-1 right rectangles (fo